SEC. 70501. TERMINATION OF PREVIOUSLY-OWNED CLEAN VEHICLE CREDIT.

This section terminates the previously-owned clean vehicle credit by amending Section 25E(g) of the Internal Revenue Code, changing the expiration date from December 31, 2032, to September 30, 2025. This means the credit will no longer be available for qualifying used clean vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025.

SEC. 70502. TERMINATION OF CLEAN VEHICLE CREDIT.

This section terminates the clean vehicle credit for new vehicles by amending Section 30D(h), changing the eligibility from vehicles "placed in service after December 31, 2032" to vehicles "acquired after September 30, 2025." It also makes conforming amendments to Section 30D(e) to remove provisions related to the now-terminated credit, including the elimination of certain requirements for critical minerals and battery components.

SEC. 70503. TERMINATION OF QUALIFIED COMMERCIAL CLEAN VEHICLES CREDIT.

This section terminates the qualified commercial clean vehicles credit by amending Section 45W(g), changing the expiration date from December 31, 2032, to September 30, 2025. This means the credit will no longer be available for qualifying commercial clean vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025.

SEC. 70504. TERMINATION OF ALTERNATIVE FUEL VEHICLE REFUELING PROPERTY CREDIT.

This section terminates the alternative fuel vehicle refueling property credit by amending Section 30C(i), changing the expiration date from December 31, 2032, to June 30, 2026. This means the credit will no longer be available for qualifying refueling property placed in service after June 30, 2026.

SEC. 70505. TERMINATION OF ENERGY EFFICIENT HOME IMPROVEMENT CREDIT.

This section terminates the energy efficient home improvement credit by amending Section 25C(h), changing the expiration date from December 31, 2032, to December 31, 2025. It also makes a conforming amendment to Section 25C(d)(2)(C) to update the efficiency criteria for oil furnaces and hot water boilers to the 2021 Energy Star standards and require they be rated for use with fuel blends containing at least 20% eligible fuel.

SEC. 70506. TERMINATION OF RESIDENTIAL CLEAN ENERGY CREDIT.

This section terminates the residential clean energy credit by amending Section 25D(h), changing the eligibility from property placed in service after December 31, 2034, to expenditures made after December 31, 2025. It also makes conforming amendments to Section 25D(g) to remove the phase-out percentages for the credit, leaving the credit at a flat 30% until its termination.

SEC. 70507. TERMINATION OF ENERGY EFFICIENT COMMERCIAL BUILDINGS DEDUCTION.

This section terminates the energy efficient commercial buildings deduction by adding a new subsection (i) to Section 179D, stating that the section shall not apply to property the construction of which begins after June 30, 2026.

SEC. 70508. TERMINATION OF NEW ENERGY EFFICIENT HOME CREDIT.

This section terminates the new energy efficient home credit by amending Section 45L(h), changing the expiration date from December 31, 2032, to June 30, 2026. This means the credit will no longer be available for qualifying new energy efficient homes acquired after June 30, 2026.

SEC. 70509. TERMINATION OF COST RECOVERY FOR ENERGY PROPERTY.

This section terminates the 5-year cost recovery period for certain energy property by amending Section 168(e)(3)(B)(vi), striking the subclause that provided for this treatment. The amendment applies to property the construction of which begins after December 31, 2024.

SEC. 70510. MODIFICATIONS OF ZERO-EMISSION NUCLEAR POWER PRODUCTION CREDIT.

This section adds restrictions to the zero-emission nuclear power production credit by amending Section 45U(c). It denies the credit to taxpayers that are "specified foreign entities" for taxable years beginning after enactment, and to "foreign-influenced entities" for taxable years beginning after two years from enactment.

SEC. 70511. TERMINATION OF CLEAN HYDROGEN PRODUCTION CREDIT.

This section terminates the clean hydrogen production credit by amending Section 45V(c)(3)(C), changing the expiration date from January 1, 2033, to January 1, 2028. This means the credit will no longer be available for hydrogen produced after January 1, 2028.

SEC. 70512. TERMINATION AND RESTRICTIONS ON CLEAN ELECTRICITY PRODUCTION CREDIT.

This section makes several changes to the clean electricity production credit. It terminates the credit for wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027, and adds restrictions denying the credit to taxpayers that are "specified foreign entities" or "foreign-influenced entities." It also defines "prohibited foreign entities" and "material assistance from a prohibited foreign entity," and denies the credit for facilities with material assistance from such entities, with thresholds phasing in from 2026 to 2030. It also denies the credit for wind and solar leasing arrangements, requires the Secretary to consider existing studies on emissions rates, expands the definition of nuclear energy communities, and adds a 6-year statute of limitations for errors related to material assistance determinations.

SEC. 70513. TERMINATION AND RESTRICTIONS ON CLEAN ELECTRICITY INVESTMENT CREDIT.

This section makes several changes to the clean electricity investment credit. It terminates the credit for wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027, and adds restrictions denying the credit to taxpayers that are "specified foreign entities" or "foreign-influenced entities." It also denies the credit for facilities with material assistance from prohibited foreign entities, adds a recapture provision for payments to prohibited foreign entities, and denies the credit for wind and solar leasing arrangements. It modifies the domestic content rules with adjusted percentages, eliminates the energy credit for certain energy property, and applies the credit to qualified fuel cell property.

SEC. 70514. PHASE-OUT AND RESTRICTIONS ON ADVANCED MANUFACTURING PRODUCTION CREDIT.

This section modifies the advanced manufacturing production credit. It adds a requirement that for integrated components, at least 65% of the total direct material costs must be attributable to primary components mined, produced, or manufactured in the United States. It phases out the credit for applicable critical minerals (other than metallurgical coal) from 2031 to 2033, terminates it for wind energy components after December 31, 2027, and for metallurgical coal after December 31, 2029. It also adds restrictions denying the credit to taxpayers that are "specified foreign entities" or "foreign-influenced entities," and denies the credit for property with material assistance from prohibited foreign entities. It expands the definition of battery module and adds metallurgical coal as an applicable critical mineral with a 2.5% credit rate.

SEC. 70515. RESTRICTION ON THE EXTENSION OF ADVANCED ENERGY PROJECT CREDIT PROGRAM.

This section restricts the advanced energy project credit program by amending Section 48C(e)(3)(C), changing the allocation of credits from "shall be increased" to "shall not be increased." This prevents the Secretary from increasing the amount of credits allocated under the program.

SEC. 70521. EXTENSION AND MODIFICATION OF CLEAN FUEL PRODUCTION CREDIT.

This section extends and modifies the clean fuel production credit. It requires that fuel be exclusively derived from feedstocks produced or grown in the United States, Mexico, or Canada, and prohibits negative emission rates. It also excludes emissions from indirect land use changes, provides for distinct emissions rates for fuels derived from animal manure, and extends the credit's expiration date from December 31, 2027, to December 31, 2029. It prevents double credit for fuel produced from a fuel for which a credit is already allowable, and adds restrictions denying the credit to taxpayers that are "specified foreign entities" or "foreign-influenced entities."

SEC. 70522. RESTRICTIONS ON CARBON OXIDE SEQUESTRATION CREDIT.

This section adds restrictions to the carbon oxide sequestration credit, denying it to taxpayers that are "specified foreign entities" or "foreign-influenced entities." It also creates parity for different uses of qualified carbon oxide by setting a single credit amount of $17 per metric ton (or $36 for certain uses) for all qualified carbon oxide, regardless of whether it is disposed of, used as a tertiary injectant, or utilized.

SEC. 70523. INTANGIBLE DRILLING AND DEVELOPMENT COSTS TAKEN INTO ACCOUNT FOR PURPOSES OF COMPUTING ADJUSTED FINANCIAL STATEMENT INCOME.

This section modifies the calculation of adjusted financial statement income (AFSI) to require that deductions for intangible drilling and development costs (IDCs) be taken into account. It reduces AFSI by the amount of IDC deductions allowed for tax purposes and disregards any depletion expense related to IDCs on the taxpayer's financial statement.

SEC. 70524. INCOME FROM HYDROGEN STORAGE, CARBON CAPTURE, ADVANCED NUCLEAR, HYDROPOWER, AND GEOTHERMAL ENERGY ADDED TO QUALIFYING INCOME OF CERTAIN PUBLICLY TRADED PARTNERSHIPS.

This section expands the definition of qualifying income for publicly traded partnerships to include income from the transportation or storage of certain fuels (including hydrogen), the generation or storage of electric power at qualified carbon capture facilities, the production of electricity from advanced nuclear facilities, the production of electricity or thermal energy using qualified energy resources, and the operation of certain energy property.

SEC. 70525. ALLOW FOR PAYMENTS TO CERTAIN INDIVIDUALS WHO DYE FUEL.

This section creates a new provision allowing for payments to individuals who remove eligible indelibly dyed diesel fuel or kerosene from a terminal. The payment is equal to the tax previously paid on the fuel, provided the fuel is exempt from taxation under section 4082(a).

SEC. 70531. MODIFICATIONS TO DE MINIMIS ENTRY PRIVILEGE FOR COMMERCIAL SHIPMENTS.

This section adds a civil penalty of up to $5,000 for the first violation and up to $10,000 for each subsequent violation for any person who enters, introduces, facilitates, or attempts to introduce an article into the United States using the de minimis entry privilege in violation of customs law. It also repeals the commercial shipment exception to the de minimis entry privilege, effective July 1, 2027.

SEC. 70601. MODIFICATION AND EXTENSION OF LIMITATION ON EXCESS BUSINESS LOSSES OF NONCORPORATE TAXPAYERS.

This section makes the limitation on excess business losses of noncorporate taxpayers permanent by removing the expiration date of January 1, 2029. It also updates the base amount for calculating the excess business loss from December 31, 2018, to December 31, 2025, and the inflation adjustment from 2017 to 2024.

SEC. 70602. TREATMENT OF PAYMENTS FROM PARTNERSHIPS TO PARTNERS FOR PROPERTY OR SERVICES.

This section modifies the treatment of payments from partnerships to partners for property or services by striking "Under regulations prescribed" and inserting "Except as provided" in Section 707(a)(2). This change removes the requirement for regulations to be prescribed for the provision to apply, making it directly applicable.

SEC. 70603. EXCESSIVE EMPLOYEE REMUNERATION FROM CONTROLLED GROUP MEMBERS AND ALLOCATION OF DEDUCTION.

This section applies the $1 million deduction limit on excessive employee remuneration to all members of a controlled group. It requires that the limit be applied to "specified covered employees" of the controlled group, and if the aggregate remuneration from all group members exceeds $1 million, the deduction limit is allocated among the members based on the proportion of remuneration each provides.

SEC. 70604. EXCISE TAX ON CERTAIN REMITTANCE TRANSFERS.

This section imposes a 1% excise tax on remittance transfers paid by the sender. The tax applies only to transfers funded with cash, money orders, cashier's checks, or similar physical instruments, and does not apply to transfers from financial institution accounts or funded with debit or credit cards. The remittance transfer provider is responsible for collecting and remitting the tax quarterly.

SEC. 70605. ENFORCEMENT PROVISIONS WITH RESPECT TO COVID-RELATED EMPLOYEE RETENTION CREDITS.

This section adds enforcement provisions for COVID-related employee retention credits (ERTC). It imposes a $1,000 penalty on COVID-ERTC promoters who fail to comply with due diligence requirements, and defines "COVID-ERTC promoter" based on the proportion of their gross receipts from ERTC-related services. It also limits credits and refunds to claims filed by January 31, 2024, extends the statute of limitations for assessment to 6 years, and expands the penalty for erroneous claims to include employment tax.

SEC. 70606. SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBER REQUIREMENT FOR AMERICAN OPPORTUNITY AND LIFETIME LEARNING CREDITS.

This section requires taxpayers to include their social security number and, for credits related to other individuals, the name and social security number of that individual, to claim the American Opportunity and Lifetime Learning credits. It also requires the taxpayer to include the employer identification number of any institution to which qualified tuition was paid. The omission of this information is treated as a mathematical or clerical error.

SEC. 70607. TASK FORCE ON THE REPLACEMENT OF DIRECT FILE.

This section appropriates $15,000,000 for the Department of the Treasury to deliver a report to Congress within 90 days on the cost of enhancing public-private partnerships for free tax filing, taxpayer opinions on government-run vs. private services, the feasibility of a new approach, and the cost of developing and running a free direct e-file tax return system. [end of text]


